  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "If $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $5y=6x$ then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\u27e8\\frac{6}{5},1\u27e9\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false.\nNotice that the point $(\\frac{6}{5},1)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $5y=6x$ since $5\\cdot 1\\ne \\frac{6}{5}\\cdot 6.$ Therefore, \n$\u27e8\\frac{6}{5},1\u27e9\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is true.\nSince $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line passing through the origin, we have that the standard matrix $T$ is singular. So, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false.\nSince  statement II is true we have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))\\ne 0.$",
